  "annotations": [
    {
      "id": "n-menu",
      "x": 940,
      "y": -170,
      "w": 390,
      "text": "The hub these twenty hang off — 4x5 on a phone, 5x4 on a tablet, one tile per activity in the registry's family order.\nThe canvas below is laid out in exactly the Menu's order, so a tile and its screen sit in the same place."
    },
    {
      "id": "n-practice",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 970,
      "w": 390,
      "text": "PRACTICE — the five that teach something new.\nAll five run inside DrillShell: one item, the 56px bar, the ? help ladder, no per-activity header. That shared frame is why six drills stopped feeling like six apps."
    },
    {
      "id": "n-svplay",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 2110,
      "w": 390,
      "text": "SvPLAY — the four games, plus DéjàRevu at the end of the row.\nThe games keep their own worlds (a road, a trading floor, rain, a vault) rather than paper. That is deliberate and worth keeping — but it is also where the 508 off-system hex values live."
    },
    {
      "id": "n-skills",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 3250,
      "w": 390,
      "text": "REVIEW + SKILLS — the drills that push production rather than recognition.\nConjugaZone is the one to study: it withholds the conjugation table until you finish, so the table is the reward instead of the gate. Study follows proof."
    },
    {
      "id": "n-user",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 4390,
      "w": 390,
      "text": "The last two Skills, then USER — the three surfaces that tell a learner where they stand.\nThese carry almost all the reward cues, and today almost all of them render in body-text ink. They are where the colour review bites hardest."
    }
  ],
  "launch": {
    "view": "canvas"
  }
}